From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: cubie-a7s: drive the J7 fan from the CPU
 thermal zones

The Cubie A7S has a fan header, J7, whose PWM line nothing drives yet.
Hang a pwm-fan on it and make it a cooling device of both CPU zones, so a
fan plugged into J7 runs by temperature instead of at whatever level its
own input floats to.

Where each value comes from

  * The header: schematic v1.10, page 16 ("KEY_LED_FAN"). J7 carries GND
    on pin 1, PJ27_PWM1-9 through R81 (1 kOhm series) on pin 2 and
    VCC5V0_SYS on pin 3; pads 4 and 5 are GND. No tachometer pin, so the
    node has no interrupts, and no switch in the 5 V supply, so there is
    no regulator to name as fan-supply.
  * The pin: PJ27 function 3 is PWM1-9 (A733 user manual V1.00, Table
    18-36, and PJ_CFG3 bits 15:12, 0011 = PWM1-9). PWM1 is the controller
    at 0x02528000 described by `arm64-dts-a733-add-the-two-pwm-controllers`;
    this patch adds its pin state and enables it. PJ26 shares the PCCR89
    clock pair with channel 9 and is the status LED as a plain GPIO, so no
    other PWM user can contend for the pair's divider.
  * The period: 40000 ns, 25 kHz, the frequency 4-wire PWM fans are built
    for. Normal polarity: there is no transistor between pin and header,
    so a high pin is a high fan input.
  * cooling-levels: off and four equal steps of 25 %.
  * The trip: one active trip per CPU zone at 60 C with 10 C hysteresis,
    in the trip-2/map1 slot that `arm64-dts-a733-idle-injection-cooling`
    left free for this patch. 60 C is above the 31-45 C idle measured on a
    board with heatsink and fan and 30 C below the 90 C passive trip, so
    the fan steps through its levels before cpufreq throttles. 10 C is
    wider than the sensor noise described in
    `thermal-sun8i-a733-filter-the-trend-fed-to-step-wise`, so noise alone
    does not switch the fan on and off. Both zones map the same fan; the
    thermal core sets it to the higher of the two requested states.

What a board without a fan sees: the channel drives a 1 kOhm resistor
into an open header and nothing else changes. pwm-fan probes without a
tachometer, as the binding allows.

Between probe and the first zone evaluation pwm-fan holds the fan at its
highest level; the first evaluation below the trip turns it off.

Checked on a board without a fan on J7: pwm-fan probes as a hwmon
device and a cooling device bound to the active trip of both CPU zones;
under load across the trip the state rose and PWM1 channel 9 drove a
40000 ns period at the duty of that level with normal polarity, and back
below the trip the state returned to 0 and the channel was disabled. A
fan on J7 itself has not been run.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
@@ -4704,3 +4704,90 @@ gpu_critical: gpu-trip-0 {
 		};
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * Fan header J7 (schematic v1.10, page 16 "KEY_LED_FAN"). Three pins:
+ * 1 GND, 2 PJ27_PWM1-9 through R81 (1 kOhm series), 3 VCC5V0_SYS; pads 4
+ * and 5 are GND. There is no tachometer pin and no switch in the supply,
+ * so the PWM line is the only control: the fan's own PWM input decides
+ * the speed, and VCC5V0_SYS has no regulator node to name as fan-supply.
+ *
+ * PJ27 function 3 is PWM1-9 (A733 user manual V1.00, Table 18-36 and
+ * PJ_CFG3 bits 15:12 = 0011). Its reset value 1111 is IO disable, so
+ * until the PWM controller probes the pin floats and the fan's input sits
+ * at whatever level the fan itself pulls it to. PJ26, the other half of
+ * the PCCR89 pair, is the status LED as a plain GPIO, so no PWM shares
+ * this channel's clock divider.
+ *
+ * pwms: channel 9, 40000 ns (25 kHz, the 4-wire PWM fan convention),
+ * normal polarity: no transistor sits between pin and header, so a high
+ * pin is a high fan input. cooling-levels: off and four equal steps of
+ * 25 %.
+ *
+ * The fan hangs off both CPU zones at trip-2/map1, the slot the thermal
+ * zones leave free for it. The cooling device takes the higher of the two
+ * requested states. 60 C sits above the 31-45 C idle of a board with
+ * heatsink and fan and 30 C below the 90 C passive trip, so the fan has
+ * that band to step through its levels before cpufreq throttles. The
+ * 10 C hysteresis is wider than the sensor noise (up to 6.3 K
+ * peak-to-peak, see thermal-sun8i-a733-filter-the-trend-fed-to-step-wise),
+ * so noise alone cannot switch the fan on and off. With nothing plugged
+ * into J7 the channel drives a 1 kOhm resistor into an open pin and
+ * nothing else changes.
+ */
+&pio {
+	pwm1_pj27_pin: pwm1-pj27-pin {
+		pins = "PJ27";
+		allwinner,pinmux = <3>;
+		function = "pwm1";
+	};
+};
+
+&pwm1 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&pwm1_pj27_pin>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+/ {
+	fan: pwm-fan {
+		compatible = "pwm-fan";
+		pwms = <&pwm1 9 40000 0>;
+		cooling-levels = <0 64 128 192 255>;
+		#cooling-cells = <2>;
+	};
+};
+
+&cpul_thermal {
+	trips {
+		cpul_fan: cpul-trip-2 {
+			temperature = <60000>;
+			type = "active";
+			hysteresis = <10000>;
+		};
+	};
+
+	cooling-maps {
+		map1 {
+			trip = <&cpul_fan>;
+			cooling-device = <&fan THERMAL_NO_LIMIT THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&cpub_thermal {
+	trips {
+		cpub_fan: cpub-trip-2 {
+			temperature = <60000>;
+			type = "active";
+			hysteresis = <10000>;
+		};
+	};
+
+	cooling-maps {
+		map1 {
+			trip = <&cpub_fan>;
+			cooling-device = <&fan THERMAL_NO_LIMIT THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+		};
+	};
+};
